TY  - GEN
TI  - fork() for Agents: Agent Kernel for Non-blocking LLM Orchestration
AU  - Chadha, Ankit
AU  - Banerjee, Pratyay
PY  - 2026
DA  - 2026/11/24
DO  - 10.48550/arXiv.2611.31727
UR  - https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.31727
L1  - https://ankitrchadha.com/papers/agent-kernel-non-blocking-llm-orchestration/agent-kernel.pdf
AB  - LLM-based agents typically execute tool calls synchronously and discard completed work upon failure. Subgraph-selective recovery, re-executing only a failed node's transitive descendants, is a well-established pattern in distributed systems (Spark, Erlang/OTP, Dryad), and recent agent runtimes (Sherlock, AgentGit, Atomix) preserve completed work through verifiers, version control, or transactional tool boundaries. We present Agent-Kernel, a non-blocking runtime that ports this pattern to the LLM content plane: its primitive, Sub-Graph Recovery (SGR), fires on a raw exception with no trained verifier or planner, resets a failed node's descendants while preserving completed siblings, and composes each parent's text into its children's prompts (context inheritance), all on a durable substrate with a formal state-preservation guarantee. Across 14,434 runs on synthetic and standard benchmarks (tau-bench, Gaia2, AutomationBench, InterruptBench), SGR reaches full recovery within budget=3 where whole-trajectory replay exhausts (100% vs. 55%), and is roughly 10× faster than an adapted Sherlock rollback that also recovers. It matches ReAct-serial accuracy within noise on linear workloads while adding recovery and parallelism (up to 12.7× speedup) where structure permits. Ablation isolates SGR: removing it collapses success to 39.4%.
